let's now consider how to find the  velocity of the center of mass of a  wheel that's rolling without slipping  down an inclined plane so let's draw a  picture here's the wheel it starts at T  equals zero and this is an angle C and  at some later time T final the wheel has  dropped a distance H along the inclined  plane and now let's figure out what is  the velocity of the center of mass of  the wheel when it gets to the bottom and  we are now going to apply our energy  arguments now the first thing we have to  realize is is a very subtle point here  is that there can be friction and we saw  that when a wheel is rolling without  slipping there can be static friction  between ethylic instant the contact  point between the wheel and the ground  there is a gravitational force and a  normal force now static friction does no  work because the point is always  instantaneously at rest and f dot d s is  zero because the object is at rest and  so the D s is zero so our static  friction does no work and therefore from  our energy principle that the external  work equals e final minus V initial we  have no external work and so our energy  is constant now how do we analyze our  energy  well I've set things up so I'm going to  choose my potential energy to be zero  when it's at the bottom and that our  initial energy is only equal to  potential energy if the wheel has a mass  and  gravitational force down the potential  energy initially is MGH the final energy  is only kinetic energy the wheel is  rolling with omega final its center of  mass is moving with V center of mass  final and we just saw that the kinetic  energy has two contributions the  translational kinetic energy one-half M  we'll just call this final squared and  we'll make that V final just to make it  easier and it has the kinetic energy of  rotation now the rolling without  slipping condition is that the  translational center of mass speed is  equal to the radius of the wheel R times  the angular speed Omega fina